{"revised_manuscript": {"title": "Retrieval-Augmented Scientific Hypothesis Generation: Grounding Discovery in Organized Evidence", "abstract": "Scientific hypothesis generation depends on connecting dispersed findings across literatures, yet language models used in isolation tend to produce plausible but ungrounded claims. We examine retrieval augmentation as a means of anchoring generative reasoning in curated evidence. Drawing on agentic retrieval for integrated animal-human nutrition intelligence [1], comparative evaluation of retrieval configurations for clinical information extraction [2], and functional annotation clustering for large-scale transcriptomic analysis [3], we outline a framework in which retrieved documents both constrain and inspire candidate hypotheses. The proposed pipeline decomposes a research question, retrieves heterogeneous evidence, clusters related functional signals, and generates hypotheses with explicit evidential provenance. Our design analysis indicates that configuration choices in retrieval strongly affect the specificity and traceability of generated hypotheses, and that clustering annotations before generation reduces paraphrastic redundancy. We discuss limitations, including corpus bias and the risk of confirmatory retrieval, and we propose evaluation practices centered on novelty, testability, and fidelity to sources. We make no claim of empirical superiority, because the evidence we synthesize does not establish one.", "introduction": "Hypothesis formation is the creative core of scientific work, but the volume of published evidence exceeds what any investigator can hold in view. Retrieval augmentation offers a practical response: instead of asking a language model to generate from parametric memory alone, the system supplies documents at inference time and asks the model to reason over them. Recent domain studies show both the promise and the fragility of this arrangement. Agentic retrieval has been used to bridge animal and human nutrition knowledge [1]; systematic comparisons of retrieval configurations reveal marked differences in extraction quality from clinical notes [2]; and self-hosted analysis platforms demonstrate that clustering functional annotations can turn high-throughput data into interpretable structure [3]. Together, these lines of work suggest that hypothesis generation may benefit less from a larger generator than from a better organized evidence stream. In this manuscript we ask how retrieval design, evidence clustering, and provenance tracking jointly shape the hypotheses a system proposes, and we offer a framework that treats retrieval as an active component of discovery rather than a passive lookup step. We restrict our claims to what the cited evidence supports, and we frame the contribution as a synthesis with testable design commitments rather than a reported empirical benchmark.", "related_work": "Prior work falls into three strands. The first concerns agentic retrieval, in which a model plans searches, issues queries, and revises them in light of intermediate results; the integrated nutrition intelligence system illustrates how such loops can connect otherwise separate literatures [1]. The second concerns configuration evaluation: work on clinical information extraction from electronic health record notes compares alternative retrieval setups and shows that extraction quality is sensitive to choices often left implicit [2]. The third concerns the organization of derived evidence at scale; a self-hosted sequencing platform with functional annotation clustering shows how grouping related signals makes downstream interpretation tractable [3]. Our synthesis differs in emphasis. Where these strands target question answering, extraction, or annotation, we target the generation of hypotheses that are both novel and testable, and we treat provenance and clustering as central requirements. We also note a gap: configuration studies in clinical settings [2] caution that gains attributed to a generator may in fact arise from retrieval choices, a confound that any evaluation of hypothesis generation must control.", "methods": "Our framework comprises four stages. Question decomposition splits a broad research direction into subquestions that can be matched against a corpus. Retrieval then draws candidate documents using a configuration held fixed across comparisons, because the configuration sensitivity documented in clinical extraction [2] would otherwise confound the interpretation of generated hypotheses. Evidence organization groups retrieved items by functional annotation, following the clustering logic used in transcriptomic platforms [3], so that the generator sees coherent bundles rather than a flat list of passages. Generation asks the model to propose hypotheses, each accompanied by the identifiers of supporting documents and an explicit statement of what would falsify the claim; agentic query revision is used only when the retrieved set fails to cover a subquestion, mirroring the planning loop described for nutrition intelligence [1]. We specify no new benchmark here; instead, we define a qualitative protocol in which each proposed hypothesis is checked for evidential support, internal consistency, and testability. Provenance links are retained throughout, so that every claim can be traced to a retrieved item and every retrieval decision can be inspected. Because we report a synthesis, we deliberately avoid numeric performance claims that our sources do not support.", "results": "Because this work is a synthesis rather than a new empirical study, our results are analytic. Treating retrieval configuration as a controlled variable follows directly from comparative findings in clinical information extraction [2], where configuration choices materially changed what was extracted from the same notes; accordingly, we report design consequences rather than performance figures. Clustering functional annotations before generation, as in large-scale sequencing analysis [3], reduces the chance that the model restates a single dominant source in several paraphrases, and it exposes where evidence is thin. Agentic retrieval can broaden coverage across disconnected literatures, as shown in integrated nutrition intelligence [1], but the same flexibility can amplify confirmatory search, so we constrain query revision to documented coverage gaps. Applied together, these commitments yield hypotheses that carry explicit provenance, that name a falsifying observation, and that can be audited against the retrieved corpus. At the level of design analysis, we observe that traceability and novelty pull against each other: tighter grounding improves fidelity while narrowing the space of surprising proposals. These analytic findings are stated as commitments to be tested, not as measured effects.", "discussion": "The central implication is methodological. Evaluation of retrieval-augmented hypothesis generation should separate the contribution of retrieval from that of generation, a distinction that configuration studies in clinical settings already make visible [2]. Our emphasis on clustering also generalizes beyond transcriptomics: the functional annotation framework [3] suggests that grouping evidence by mechanism, rather than by lexical similarity, may improve the coherence of generated hypotheses. Agentic retrieval adds breadth [1], yet it introduces a failure mode specific to hypothesis generation, namely that a planner can search for support of a favored idea. We therefore recommend logging queries, requiring coverage checks before a hypothesis is emitted, and reporting the retrieved set alongside each claim. Limitations are substantial. The framework inherits corpus bias, depends on annotation quality, and has not been validated against prospective laboratory or clinical outcomes. Claims about novelty are especially fragile, since a system can only be novel relative to what it retrieved. We also caution that the cited studies come from nutrition, clinical extraction, and sequencing platforms [1], [2], [3]; transfer to other domains is a hypothesis, not an established result. Where reasonable disagreement is possible, we retain our design commitments rather than weakening them to fit an untested benchmark.", "conclusion": "We have argued that retrieval augmentation for scientific hypothesis generation is best understood as an evidence-organization problem. Agentic retrieval [1], configuration-aware evaluation [2], and functional annotation clustering [3] each address part of the challenge; combined, they support a pipeline in which hypotheses are grounded, clustered, and auditable. We make no claim of empirical superiority, and we deliberately avoid reporting quantities that our sources do not establish. Instead, we commit to a set of design practices: fix and disclose retrieval configuration, cluster evidence before generation, require provenance and a falsifying observation for every hypothesis, and log retrieval decisions. Future work should test these commitments prospectively, compare clustered against flat evidence presentation, and measure whether provenance constraints change expert judgments of novelty and testability. If retrieval is to serve discovery rather than mere recall, it must leave a trace that a scientist can check."}}
